\begin{abstract}
Eye movements, blinks, and the corneo-retinal standing potential leak into scalp
electroencephalography (EEG) and can co-vary with task structure, so a classifier that appears
to decode a cognitive state may instead be exploiting condition-correlated ocular activity.
Existing defenses either remove ocular signal or model it within a single eye-tracked recording;
they do not provide a standardized post hoc audit for completed EEG decoding datasets that lack
target eye tracking. We define an ocular-subspace auditing framework. A linear EEG-to-gaze
readout, trained where synchronized gaze exists, induces a locked ocular subspace: the
orthogonal projection onto the span of its weights. The auditor operates on all available
channels; here we evaluate a full 64-channel montage and, as an explicitly specified sparse
case, the 30-channel ERP-CORE subset. The Ocular Predictability Index (OPI) is the
cross-validated area under the curve (AUC) of a paradigm-matched decoder applied to the ocular
projection, minus a within-subject label-permutation null. OPI should therefore be interpreted
as label information decodable from the learned ocular subspace, not as a literal fraction of
the full-EEG decoding result.

We evaluate the framework primarily in a physically grounded EEG-plus-gaze simulator, where
the true ocular contribution is known, and include a limited real-EEG negative-control
demonstration on PhysioNet \texttt{eegmmidb}, which lacks eye tracking. The instrument includes
a reliability gate that returns ``inconclusive'' when gaze cannot be reconstructed with
sufficient competence in the target montage, an empirically measured bias envelope, and a
neural-leakage diagnostic based on the principal-angle overlap between the cognitive
topography and the ocular subspace. In simulation, OPI is 0.43 on saccade-coupled ocular data
and 0.20 on a condition-correlated blink artifact, versus at most 0.03 on negative controls,
and rises monotonically with artifact--label coupling. A lateralized neural source decodable
from full EEG (AUC 0.79) is not decodable from the ocular projection (OPI 0.007, not
significant). Neural leakage tracks the topographic overlap diagnostic (Spearman correlation
0.95). These results support the feasibility of a frozen, montage-matched ocular-subspace
audit where source and montage conditions are validated, while emphasizing that real-world
transfer validity remains to be established with synchronized EEG-plus-gaze target datasets.
\end{abstract}

