\section{Introduction}

A decoding result is only as trustworthy as the signal it rests on. In EEG, a recurring
threat to construct validity is that a classifier credited with reading a cognitive
state (attention, working-memory load, target detection, the meaning of a read
word) is partly reading the eyes. The eye is an electrical dipole, cornea-positive and
retina-negative, with a standing potential of roughly $0.4$--$1.0$\,mV~\cite{malmivuo1995bioelectromagnetism};
its rotations, together with blinks and the broadband saccadic spike potential at saccade
onset~\cite{keren2010saccadic}, project to frontal and periocular electrodes with amplitudes
that dwarf cortical signals. Crucially, the field of an eye movement
depends on each electrode's \emph{position} relative to the eyes: its sign reverses across the
midline (a dipole) and its magnitude falls with distance, and a blink imposes a different,
symmetric pattern, so the contamination an electrode sees is montage- and artifact-specific.
Because where and when a person looks is rarely independent of the experimental condition,
this ocular activity is \emph{condition-correlated}: it can be decoded in lockstep with the
cognitive label and inflate apparent accuracy. The danger is the machine-learning failure
mode of shortcut learning: exploiting an incidental correlate rather than the intended
construct~\cite{geirhos2020shortcut}.

The standard defenses are incomplete. Independent component analysis and regression
\emph{remove} ocular components~\cite{jung2000removing,plochl2012combining}, but removal is
imperfect and leaves a residual whose decodable content is unknown; overweighting the
saccadic spike before ICA improves this but still targets removal, not
measurement~\cite{dimigen2020optimizing,piontonachini2019iclabel}. Deconvolution frameworks
rigorously model ocular and overlap effects, but in practice within a single, carefully
co-registered, eye-tracked recording~\cite{ehinger2019unfold,dimigen2011coregistration}.
None of these directly answers the question an author or a reviewer often faces about a
finished result: \emph{is task-label information still decodable from a signal subspace that is
predictive of ocular activity?} This framing is deliberately weaker than a causal
decomposition: the goal is to quantify label decodability in an ocular-predictive projection,
not to assign a literal fraction of the full-EEG decoding result to the eyes. This is especially
relevant for the thousands of datasets recorded with no eye-tracker at all.

The concern is not hypothetical. Mostert \emph{et al.}~\cite{mostert2018eye} showed that a
visual working-memory ``representation'' could be partly explained by systematic eye movements,
and constructed a bespoke, within-experiment control: if the target variable cannot be decoded
from simultaneously recorded gaze, the neural result is safer. Rotaru
\emph{et al.}~\cite{rotaru2024decoding} found that EEG decoders of the spatial focus of
\emph{auditory} attention overfit to eye-gaze and per-trial fingerprints, dropping to chance
once gaze was controlled. More broadly, the field increasingly rewards auditing its methods and
benchmarks for inflated inference~\cite{eklund2016cluster,kapoor2023leakage,brookshire2024leakage,chaibubneto2018permutations}.
What is missing, to our knowledge, is a \emph{transferable instrument}: a frozen,
montage-matched ocular-subspace probe that can be pointed at an EEG decoding dataset, including
one with no target gaze recording, and that returns an interpretable audit statistic together
with an honest statement of when it cannot be trusted.

This paper defines and evaluates that instrument. Our contributions are: (i) a locked,
linear ocular-subspace operator and an \emph{Ocular Predictability Index} (OPI) with a
within-subject permutation null and a full-EEG ceiling; (ii) a three-tier reliability gate
that returns ``inconclusive'' rather than a false clean bill of health; (iii) an empirically
measured two-sided bias envelope, so the index can be interpreted with a stated uncertainty
margin; and (iv) a computable neural-leakage diagnostic based on the principal angle between
the cognitive topography and the ocular subspace. We validate these components against ground
truth in a physically grounded simulator and include a limited real-EEG negative-control
demonstration. Because the real EEG dataset used here lacks synchronized gaze, the present
study should be read as simulation-calibrated evidence for the audit framework, not as a final
demonstration of real-world gaze-trained transfer.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Design and rationale}

The instrument is deliberately linear. Although a deep network is one way to learn an
EEG-to-gaze readout where synchronized eye-tracking is
available~\cite{kastrati2021eegeyenet,hollenstein2018zuco}, the object that is frozen and
shipped here is a linear readout and the orthogonal projection onto the span of its weights.
We therefore implement and evaluate exactly that object. Because ground truth about the true
ocular contribution is available only when gaze is recorded or simulated, the primary
validation uses a forward-model simulator with known ocular and neural sources. A real-EEG
analysis on PhysioNet \texttt{eegmmidb} is included as a negative-control and cautionary
demonstration, but not as a full validation of gaze-trained transfer, because that dataset
does not contain synchronized eye tracking.

The auditor uses \emph{every} channel a dataset provides; dimensionality is never silently
reduced. We report a full 64-channel 10--10 montage as primary and treat the 30-channel
ERP-CORE set as an \emph{explicitly specified subset} of it, used only to characterize how
channel count and near-eye frontal coverage affect the auditor. In this manuscript, the
simulator supplies the ground-truth validation setting, ERP CORE defines the sparse
30-channel montage used in the simulated restriction, and PhysioNet \texttt{eegmmidb} supplies
the no-eye-tracker real-EEG negative control. EEGEyeNet and ZuCo are cited as appropriate
synchronized EEG-plus-gaze resources for training and future real-data transfer validation,
but they are not presented here as completed empirical validation datasets. Source amplitudes
are taken from the electrooculography and saccade literature.

\subsection{The locked ocular-subspace operator}

Let $\mathbf{X}_{\mathrm{src}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ be source EEG (trials $\times$
channels) for which ocular ground truth $\mathbf{Y}_{\mathrm{src}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}$
is available ($q$ outputs: horizontal and vertical gaze, a saccade channel, and a blink
indicator). A ridge readout
$\mathbf{W}=\arg\min_{\mathbf W}\lVert \mathbf{Y}_{\mathrm{src}}-\mathbf{X}_{\mathrm{src}}\mathbf{W}\rVert_F^2+\lambda\lVert\mathbf{W}\rVert_F^2$
maps EEG to ocular state. The \emph{ocular subspace} is the column space of $\mathbf{W}$, and
the locked operator is the orthogonal projection onto it,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{P}=\mathbf{U}\mathbf{U}^{\top},\qquad \mathbf{U}=\text{left singular vectors of }\mathbf{W},
\label{eq:proj}
\end{equation}
a symmetric idempotent $p\times p$ matrix ($\mathbf{P}^2=\mathbf{P}$). Projecting target EEG,
$\mathbf{X}\mapsto\mathbf{X}\mathbf{P}$, retains only the component lying in the ocular
readout's span. The gaze readout is trained only on source data with synchronized gaze, and the operator is
fixed by \eqref{eq:proj}. For a target with a different montage, the readout is re-fit \emph{on
source data restricted to that montage} (montage-matched, no periocular interpolation); the
operator is thus frozen per montage and applied unchanged to every target, never altered by
target data or target labels.

\subsection{The Ocular Predictability Index}

A paradigm-matched cognitive decoder (a ridge linear classifier) is trained and tested under
strictly subject-wise cross-validation on the projected data $\mathbf{X}\mathbf{P}$, yielding a
macro-averaged AUC. The OPI subtracts a within-subject label-permutation null (labels shuffled
within each subject, $B=200$):
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{OPI}=\mathrm{AUC}_{\mathrm{proj}}-\overline{\mathrm{AUC}}_{\mathrm{null}},
\end{equation}
and is reported alongside the full-EEG AUC as a ceiling and a permutation $p$-value. The
ocular-subspace operator is learned without target cognitive labels; the downstream audit
decoder uses those labels only to quantify how much label information survives in the ocular
projection.

OPI is not a causal fraction of the full-EEG decoding result. It is a post hoc audit statistic:
the amount by which labels remain decodable from an ocular-predictive projection above a
within-subject permutation baseline. A high OPI therefore indicates that task labels are
available in a signal component aligned with the learned ocular subspace; it does not by
itself prove that the original full-EEG classifier used the same component, nor does it
partition the full-EEG AUC into ocular and neural percentages.

\subsection{Reliability gate and bias envelope}

\emph{Reliability} is the subject-wise cross-validated gaze-reconstruction $R^2$ of the readout
restricted to the target montage, computed on \emph{source} data with synchronized gaze
(leave-one-subject-out over source subjects), never on the target. Two montages are
\emph{matched} when they share the channels the readout relies on, chiefly near-eye frontal
coverage, so this is a montage-level competence score rather than a subject-level one. Transfer
to an unseen target rests on one stated assumption: the linear ocular-to-scalp map is set by
electrode geometry and volume conduction from a common ocular generator, so a readout that
reconstructs gaze across held-out source subjects on a montage should do so for a new subject on
the same montage. A no-eye-tracker target thus inherits the score of its matched source montage;
where none matches (for example, too few frontal channels to recover gaze), the gate returns
\emph{inconclusive} rather than a number.

Thresholds are derived empirically from the reliability--bias relationship in the simulation
calibration grid and partition results into \emph{confirmatory}, \emph{exploratory}, and
\emph{inconclusive} tiers. These thresholds should be treated as calibration thresholds for
the present simulator and montage family rather than universal constants; external validation
on synchronized EEG-plus-gaze datasets is required before they are used as fixed field-wide
cutoffs. The \emph{bias envelope} is measured, not assumed: where ground-truth gaze exists, we
compare the auditor's OPI to the OPI computed directly from true gaze across the saccade-amplitude
range and report the signed deviation as a two-sided margin.

\subsection{The neural-leakage diagnostic}

The instrument's central risk is that the projection absorbs genuine neural activity that
happens to be gaze-correlated, inflating the index. We bound this. For a target whose cognitive
decoder has contrast topography $\mathbf{t}$, define the overlap
\begin{equation}
s=\frac{\lVert\mathbf{P}\mathbf{t}\rVert}{\lVert\mathbf{t}\rVert}=\cos\theta,
\label{eq:overlap}
\end{equation}
the cosine of the principal angle $\theta$ between $\mathbf{t}$ and the ocular subspace. Here
$s$ is a linear topographic-overlap diagnostic: it gives the fraction of the linear contrast
topography lying in the ocular subspace, and is $0$ when the two are orthogonal. Turning $s$ into
decodable leakage further depends on the decoder, noise, and signal amplitude, so rather than a
closed-form bound we report $s$ per audit and show empirically that leakage is monotone in $s$.

\subsection{Grounded EEG+gaze simulator}

Ground-truth validation uses a forward-model simulator on a full 64-channel 10--10
montage; the 30 ERP-CORE electrodes~\cite{kappenman2021erpcore} are an explicitly labelled
subset of these 64, so the auditor is exercised on all 64 channels and
the 30-channel case is a deliberately studied restriction, never a silent reduction. Per-trial
channel patterns are sums of physically grounded source topographies: a horizontal corneo-retinal field
($\approx 16\,\mu$V per degree), a vertical/blink frontopolar field, a focal periocular saccadic
spike scaling with saccade size~\cite{keren2010saccadic}, an $\approx 80\,\mu$V frontopolar blink,
and a lateralized posterior N2pc-like neural component ($\approx 1.8\,\mu$V)~\cite{luck1994n2pc},
on spatially correlated background noise. An artifact is coupled to the cognitive label with
tunable strength, implementing the condition-correlated premise. Scenarios include
saccade-coupled ocular and \emph{blink-coupled} ocular (two positive controls testing distinct
artifact types), covert attention (lateralized posterior neural source, central gaze), focal
motor imagery, label shuffle, and a neutral baseline.

For reproducibility: each trial is a single channel-space feature vector, and each source is a
fixed Gaussian scalp topography scaled by a per-trial amplitude, added to a shared low-rank
smooth field plus per-channel white noise ($\approx 3\,\mu$V each). The coupling $\rho$ sets the
probability that gaze (or, for blinks, the blink rate) follows the label. The readout is trained
on 20 source subjects $\times$ 200 trials at $8^\circ$ saccades; each evaluation cell is 12
subjects $\times$ 120 trials over 12 fixed-seed studies (error bars are their SD), with ridge
$\lambda{=}1$ fixed (not tuned in-fold), leave-one-subject-out macro AUC, and a $B{=}200$
within-subject label-permutation null. Complete generative equations and parameters are in the
released code.

\subsection{Real data}

We use PhysioNet \texttt{eegmmidb} (all 64 channels, BCI2000, 160\,Hz, EDF+; 8
subjects)~\cite{schalk2004bci2000,goldberger2000physionet}; no channels are dropped. Because
\texttt{eegmmidb} has no eye-tracker, this analysis cannot validate the proposed
gaze-trained ocular-subspace transfer directly. Instead, it serves two narrower purposes:
first, to test whether the audit logic behaves sensibly on a real no-eye-tracker EEG dataset
under a contrast with no expected differential gaze; and second, to illustrate why a
channel-proxy subspace is not an adequate substitute for a readout trained on synchronized
gaze.

For this demonstration, a proxy subspace is estimated from the eyes-open baseline run of four
\emph{source} subjects and applied to four held-out \emph{target} subjects' motor runs. We
audit two contrasts: left-versus-right imagined movement, where no differential gaze is
expected and the desired result is a near-null ocular projection, and rest-versus-movement,
which is included as a cautionary case because frontal non-ocular neural activity may be
retained by a proxy subspace.

\subsection{Implementation}

The auditor, simulator, and analyses are implemented in R (CPU-only). AUC is computed by the
rank statistic and cross-checked against the \texttt{pROC} package~\cite{robin2011proc} to
$<10^{-6}$. The trained auditor is small and CPU-deployable. The deployment vision, applying
the frozen instrument across public decoding benchmarks
(e.g.,~\cite{kappenman2021erpcore,gifford2022thingseeg2,arico2014p300jitter,korczowski2019bi2015a},
accessed via MOABB~\cite{jayaram2018moabb,tangermann2012bcicompetitioniv}), is discussed in
Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

\section{Results}

\subsection{The operator behaves as specified}

Unit tests confirm the rank-based AUC matches \texttt{pROC} to $<10^{-6}$; the projector of
\eqref{eq:proj} is symmetric, idempotent, and correct-rank; and end-to-end the OPI is large when
the cognitive signal lies in the ocular subspace and null when it lies outside. The trained
auditors had ocular-subspace rank 4 on the simulator and rank 2 on real \texttt{eegmmidb}.

\subsection{Graded sensitivity and specificity}

Across 12 independent simulated studies per cell on the full 64-channel montage, the OPI cleanly
separates ocular leakage from neural signal (Table~\ref{tab:battery}). The two positive controls
test \emph{distinct artifact types}: saccade-coupled ocular gives OPI $=0.429$ with projected
AUC equal to the full-EEG ceiling (0.929), and a condition-correlated \emph{eye-blink} artifact
gives OPI $=0.198$ (projected AUC 0.697), both significant in 100\% of studies. The auditor
therefore detects blink contamination as well as saccade contamination. Every negative control
is near-null in magnitude: motor imagery 0.031, label-shuffle 0.007, covert attention 0.007,
neutral $-0.003$; their envelope sets an empirical \emph{calibrated neural-leakage margin} of
$\approx 0.05$ that the index must clear beyond the bare permutation null. Motor imagery reaches
significance in 42\% of studies---a tight within-subject null plus a small sensorimotor--ocular
overlap---yet its OPI of 0.031 sits below this margin, which is why the decision rule is the
margin, not bare significance. Decisively, the covert-attention contrast is decodable from full
EEG (ceiling AUC 0.79) yet not from the ocular projection (OPI 0.007, $p=0.48$): the auditor
does not absorb lateralized posterior neural signal.

