\subsection{A headline-analog audit is gated honestly}

To emulate the deployment case we audited a simulated ERP-CORE-like N2pc target on the sparse
montage carrying a sub-$0.2^\circ$ condition-correlated ocular residual of the kind that
survives strict eye-movement rejection. The OPI was positive and significant (0.150, ceiling
AUC 0.777, $p<0.005$), \emph{but the reliability gate returned ``inconclusive''} ($R^2=0.429$,
below the exploratory threshold). The instrument withholds certification of a positive,
significant index because the montage and regime cannot reconstruct gaze well enough to trust
it: the intended ``inconclusive by design'' safeguard, not a false alarm.

\subsection{Real-EEG negative control}

On real 64-channel \texttt{eegmmidb} with a proxy subspace frozen from independent subjects'
eyes-open baseline (Table~\ref{tab:real}), the left-versus-right imagery contrast, where the
eyes have no reason to differ systematically, is weakly decodable from full EEG (AUC 0.601)
but not from the projected subspace (OPI $-0.023$, $p=0.998$). This supports the negative-control
logic: when differential ocular structure is not expected, the projected signal does not
produce a positive audit.

The rest-versus-move contrast is included as a cautionary case rather than as evidence of
ocular contamination. The proxy subspace retains decodable information (OPI $+0.114$,
$p=0.002$), but because the subspace was not trained on synchronized gaze, it may contain
frontal neural activity that cannot be separated from ocular activity. This result motivates
the proposed design choice: a deployable auditor should use a source readout trained on
synchronized EEG-plus-gaze data, not a no-gaze frontal-channel proxy.

