\item[b] Cautionary: a no-eye-tracker channel proxy may retain frontal neural signal.
\end{tablenotes}
\end{threeparttable}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

We have defined a frozen, montage-matched ocular-subspace audit framework and evaluated it
primarily in a ground-truth simulator. Under the simulated conditions tested here, the audit
has the properties required of a useful post hoc diagnostic: it is sensitive to more than one
artifact type (OPI 0.43 for saccade-coupled and 0.20 for blink-coupled contamination),
specific under neural and shuffled controls, graded with artifact--label coupling,
conservative within the measured bias envelope, and self-aware through a reliability gate
that can return ``inconclusive.'' The framework also exposes its main residual failure mode:
genuine neural signal can enter the ocular projection when the cognitive topography overlaps
the ocular subspace, and that risk is summarized by the principal-angle overlap $s$.

The central interpretation is therefore not that OPI gives the literal fraction of a decoding
result caused by the eyes. Rather, OPI measures whether task-label information remains
decodable from a source-trained ocular-predictive subspace, above a within-subject permutation
baseline. This distinction is important for construct validity: a positive OPI identifies a
plausible ocular shortcut that could support or inflate a decoding result, while a low OPI
reduces but does not eliminate concerns about other non-neural confounds.

\emph{Relation to prior work.} The instrument extends the logic of within-experiment gaze
controls, such as Mostert \emph{et al.}~\cite{mostert2018eye}, and the single-domain diagnosis
of gaze-related decoder bias in Rotaru \emph{et al.}~\cite{rotaru2024decoding}. Its intended
contribution is not another ocular-removal method, but a post hoc audit: a way to ask whether
target labels are decodable from a source-trained ocular-predictive projection when the target
EEG itself lacks gaze recordings. It therefore complements, rather than replaces, ocular
removal~\cite{jung2000removing,dimigen2020optimizing} and within-recording
deconvolution~\cite{ehinger2019unfold}. Where confound-quantification in machine learning
typically probes a measured nuisance variable~\cite{chaibubneto2018permutations}, the proposed
framework attempts to recover an ocular-predictive subspace from synchronized source data and
then tests whether target labels survive in that subspace. The residual back-door through
which this could mislead---neural leakage into the ocular projection---is diagnosed by the
overlap of \eqref{eq:overlap}.

\emph{Toward a field-wide audit.} A field-wide ocular-leakage audit remains a future
application rather than a result of the present paper. The natural next step is to train and
validate montage-matched readouts on synchronized EEG-plus-gaze resources such as EEGEyeNet
and ZuCo, then apply the frozen operators to public decoding benchmarks accessed through
MOABB~\cite{jayaram2018moabb}. Each benchmark should be paired, where possible, with a
gaze- or EOG-bearing anchor so that audit verdicts are externally ground-truthable. The
competence boundary is central to this deployment: a benchmark on which gaze cannot be
reconstructed should be reported as ``inconclusive,'' not falsely cleared.

\subsection{Limitations}

The primary limitation is that the strongest validation in this paper is simulation-based.
Measuring signed bias requires knowing the true ocular contribution, which is why the
simulator is central; however, the simulator is necessarily simplified. It uses spatial
feature vectors rather than full spatiotemporal windows, fixed Gaussian scalp topographies,
and a linear generative structure aligned with the linear projection used by the auditor.
Future work should test robustness to electrode-position jitter, individual anatomy,
preprocessing differences, montage noise, temporal dynamics, rank mismatch, and source--target
domain shift.

Second, the real-EEG demonstration is intentionally limited. PhysioNet \texttt{eegmmidb}
lacks synchronized gaze, so the proxy-subspace analysis validates only the negative-control
logic and illustrates the danger of no-gaze channel proxies. It does not establish that a
gaze-trained source readout transfers reliably to independent real target datasets. That
claim requires held-out validation in synchronized EEG-plus-gaze datasets, ideally including
both large-saccade and near-fixation regimes.

Third, the reliability thresholds reported here are empirical calibration thresholds from the
present simulation grid, not universal constants. They should be re-estimated or externally
validated before being used as fixed decision cutoffs across paradigms, montages, or
preprocessing pipelines.

Fourth, the overlap score $s$ summarizes only linear topographic overlap. Its monotonic
relationship to decodable leakage is shown empirically under the linear-subspace model, but
the translation from overlap to leakage depends on decoder class, signal-to-noise ratio,
source amplitude, and temporal feature construction. Finally, OPI is specific to ocular
subspace confounding; it will not detect non-ocular shortcuts such as repeated-stimulus
fingerprints, trial-order leakage, subject-identity leakage, or other nuisance variables.

\section{Conclusion}

EEG decoding results are vulnerable to ocular shortcuts when eye movements or blinks co-vary
with task labels, especially in datasets recorded without target eye tracking. We propose a
montage-matched ocular-subspace audit that asks a focused question: whether task labels remain
decodable from a source-trained ocular-predictive projection above a within-subject
permutation baseline. In simulation, where the true ocular contribution is known, the OPI is
sensitive to saccade and blink contamination, near-null under neural and shuffled controls,
graded with artifact--label coupling, and interpretable with a reliability gate and
neural-leakage diagnostic. The real no-eye-tracker EEG analysis provides a limited
negative-control demonstration and motivates the need for synchronized-gaze source training,
but it does not by itself establish real-world transfer validity. The next validation step is
therefore clear: train and test the frozen operators on held-out synchronized EEG-plus-gaze
datasets, then deploy the audit to public EEG decoding benchmarks with an explicit
``inconclusive'' verdict wherever montage competence is insufficient.

